      "urpx:forRatePlanModifier": {
        "@id": "sdge:care"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "sdge:cc-care-2025-10-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "sdge:cc-care-2025-10-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing carries five pieces of the CARE benefit. Four are per-unit amounts: the Wildfire Fund Charge exemption ($0.00591 per kWh), the Department of Water Resources Bond Charge exemption (currently $0.00000), the CARE Surcharge exemption ($0.00864 per kWh, or $0.01062 on Schedule EV-TOU, carried as the negative of the composed base plan's own CARE Surcharge Price) and the Base Services Charge reduction from $0.79343 to $0.19713 per day. The fifth is the percentage discount, and it is modeled here. Sheet 1 sets the residential CARE discount at 35 percent, applied as a fixed CARE line-item discount pursuant to D.24-05-028, and Sheet 2 states it as 0.35000 applied to the total bill less any applicable exemptions, minimum bill amount and base services charge, as calculated for bundled service customers. The price sdge:care-price-bill-discount-2025-10-01 carries that as a urpx:multiplier of -0.35, and its urpx:basedOnPrice names the CARE-discountable urpx:PriceSummary of each of the nine base plans this overlay declares itself applicable to, so the discount resolves against whichever of them the customer is on. Each of those summaries is the Sheet 2 base written out as a list of that plan's own prices: it names the energy, delivery and surcharge components, with the Public Purpose Programs Charge net of the CARE Surcharge, and it omits the base services charge or minimum bill together with the CARE Surcharge, the Department of Water Resources Bond Charge and the California Wildfire Fund Charge, which this overlay exempts separately. The lists run from 10 members on Schedule DR to 24 on Schedule EV-TOU-5. Sheet 4 Special Condition 8 sanctions that shape, permitting the CARE discount to be reflected either through separately stated discounted rates for each applicable service schedule or, alternatively, as an overall discount to the otherwise calculated customer, demand and energy charges. The claim stays partial, and five published provisions are why. The alternative form of benefit (b) on Sheet 1, a 50 percent minimum bill in place of a reduced base services charge, which is the form the benefit takes on Schedule EV-TOU, a plan this overlay declares itself applicable to and which has a minimum bill rather than a base services charge; that plan's rate sheet sets the minimum bill at $0.413 per day and halves it to $0.207 per day for CARE, Medical Baseline and FERA customers, while this overlay instead delivers a base services charge credit against a charge Schedule EV-TOU does not have. The Non-Residential CARE structure on Sheet 2, with its own 0.35000 discount and its own exemption list. The Medical Baseline co-benefits that Sheet 2 extends to CARE Medical Baseline customers under Schedule E-MEDICAL pursuant to D.25-09-006, for which no filing exists in this corpus. Sheet 8 Special Condition 20 PEV submetering, under which the CARE discount applies to the submetered PEV charging load as well as to the load at the primary meter where the equipment meets the D.22-08-024 submetering protocol. And the Special Condition 4 income table ($42,300 for a one or two person household rising to $108,300 for eight, plus $11,000 for each additional person), since eligibility is expressed only as residential customer kind plus enrollment in the CARE program. The remaining special conditions are out of scope rather than missing: the carry-through of the otherwise applicable schedule's own conditions (1), application and recertification (2), commencement (3), verification (5), backbilling (6), notice of change (7), the above-400-percent-of-baseline documentation and 600 percent retention conditions (9), the single-family and submetered-tenant location and qualification rules (10 through 13), and the facility certification rules for non-profit group living, Homekey and agricultural employee housing (14 through 19) are program-administration procedures rather than rates.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-09-21T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
